<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>

<autodoc>
  
  <PreData />
  
  <Field name="RootnameToProcess">
    
    <short>rootname</short>
    
    <type>CH</type>
    
    <usage>Rootname of alternative stack to process (without a or b for dual axis)</usage>
    
    <tooltip />
    
    <manpage>Enter rootname of the alternative stack to process, omitting the extension, and omitting a or b for a dual-axis data set.  Both axes of a dual-axis set are processed unless the \fB-axis\fR option is entered, but even in that case, omit a or b from this entry.</manpage>
  
  </Field>
  
  <Field name="EvenAndOddPairs">
    
    <short>evenodd</short>
    
    <type>B</type>
    
    <usage>Process even and odd aligned sums from Alignframes</usage>
    
    <tooltip />
    
    <manpage>Process even and odd aligned sums from Alignframes(1), or any pair of stacks with the data set rootname followed by &quot;_even&quot; and &quot;_odd&quot;.  You cannot use this option for a dual-axis data set; in that case you would have to enter the \fB-rootname\fR option separately for the even or odd stacks and do two separate processing runs.  Note that in order for the even and odd stacks to have valid dual-axis names, you should enter 2 for the \fB-evenodd\fR option to Alignframes(1).</manpage>
  
  </Field>
  
  <Field name="AxisToProcess">
    
    <short>axis</short>
    
    <type>CH</type>
    
    <usage>Axis of dual-axis data set to process instead of both axes</usage>
    
    <tooltip />
    
    <manpage>Axis of dual-axis data set to process.  Enter a, A, b, or B to process just a single axis for a dual-axis set.</manpage>
  
  </Field>
  
  <Field name="PreprocessForExtremes">
    
    <short>preproc</short>
    
    <type>I</type>
    
    <usage>1 to remove extreme values in raw stack, 2 to run Archiveorig also</usage>
    
    <tooltip />
    
    <manpage>Enter 1 (or any non-zero value) to remove extreme values in the raw stack with the &quot;eraser&quot; command file; enter 2 to run Archiveorig(1) afterwards to archive the original stack.</manpage>
  
  </Field>
  
  <Field name="CorrectCTF">
    
    <short>ctf</short>
    
    <type>B</type>
    
    <usage>Correct CTF in the aligned stack</usage>
    
    <tooltip />
    
    <manpage>Correct CTF in aligned stack with the &quot;ctfcorrection&quot; command file. The choice of whether to use the GPU is determined from the &quot;tilt&quot; command file, which overrides the value in the &quot;ctfcorrection&quot; command file.</manpage>
  
  </Field>
  
  <Field name="EraseFiducials">
    
    <short>erase</short>
    
    <type>B</type>
    
    <usage>Erase gold in aligned stack</usage>
    
    <tooltip />
    
    <manpage>Erase gold in aligned stack with the &quot;golderaser&quot; command file.</manpage>
  
  </Field>
  
  <Field name="FilterIn2D">
    
    <short>filter</short>
    
    <type>B</type>
    
    <usage>Filter in 2-D</usage>
    
    <tooltip />
    
    <manpage>Filter images in 2-D using the command file for running Mtffilter(1).</manpage>
  
  </Field>
  
  <Field name="TrimVolume">
    
    <short>trim</short>
    
    <type>B</type>
    
    <usage>Run Trimvol on the reconstruction using previous parameters</usage>
    
    <tooltip />
    
    <manpage>Run Trimvol(1) on the reconstruction using parameters stored in the Etomo &quot;.edf&quot; file.  If Postprocessing was just run in the Etomo interface, be sure to Postpone or otherwise close the Postprocessing panel so that current parameters are saved in the &quot;.edf&quot; file.  This command should work even if the trimming was done in a batch run and the data set has not yet been opened in Etomo.  This option cannot be used for a dual-axis data set.  The full reconstruction file for the main data stack must still be present.</manpage>
  
  </Field>
  
  <Field name="CleanUpIntermediates">
    
    <short>clean</short>
    
    <type>B</type>
    
    <usage>Remove the aligned stack, and the full reconstruction if trimming</usage>
    
    <tooltip />
    
    <manpage>When the reconstruction is done, remove the aligned stack; and when trimming is done, remove the untrimmed reconstruction.</manpage>
  
  </Field>
  
  <Field name="NumberOfProcessors">
    
    <short>procs</short>
    
    <type>I</type>
    
    <usage>Target or expected number of CPUs or GPUs (default 8, or 1 if GPU is used)</usage>
    
    <tooltip />
    
    <manpage>Use this option to specify the number of processors or GPUs that you expect to run the command files on.  If it is 1, no processing is divided into chunks.  A higher value is passed directly to Splittilt(1) and used to determine the maximum number of views to pass to Splitcorrection(1). The default is 8 unless the Tilt command file contains the option to use the GPU, in which case it is 1.</manpage>
  
  </Field>
  
  <Field name="JustRestoreInitialSet">
    
    <short>restore</short>
    
    <type>B</type>
    
    <usage>Restore files to original state if necessary, and exit</usage>
    
    <tooltip />
    
    <manpage>When the processing is stopped or fails at some point, files will be left in a swapped state, with the alternate stack(s) having the regular set name.  With this option, the program will restore files if they are in such a state, then exit without making any command files.  Without this option, the program will detect whether files are swapped, swap them back, and make new command files.</manpage>
  
  </Field>
  
  <Field name="usage">
    
    <short>help</short>
    
    <type>B</type>
    
    <usage>Print help output</usage>
    
    <tooltip />
    
    <manpage />
  
  </Field>

</autodoc>
